			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2771729">
									

<p>I hate to always harp on this particular weirdly-common Foundation interviewer tic, but the doctor has been talking to a cat with a tv for a head, one that they didn't even know they could communicate with in the first place, for <strong>a minute and a half</strong> before he starts getting mad at it. (I read the conversation out loud and timed how long it took for him to start getting agitated, when he says &quot;Don't change the subject.&quot;) It's pretty clear that this entity isn't something that's capable of fireside chats and pointed discourse; if anything, the interviewing doc should be much more supportive and engaged in order to get more information from this bizarre combination of animal and electronics. Conversations with this thing aren't going to be easy, and frankly, the researchers should be happy to be able to have them at all, so I'm not sure why they're so angry about it.</p>

							</div>

								
